From: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Subject: [PATCH 64/64] cuda/hip: unpark the rms_norm pre-add fold at one row

The residual-add fold (0008) has been parked since round 49 because it makes
the perplexity gate nondeterministic - the same binary, the same command and
the same allocation read up to nine distinct values across loads, and one
trusted-runner draw at +1.173% threw away an otherwise verified 1.9443 score.
Six mechanisms were proposed and measured dead across rounds 43b-47c.

The defect needs more than one row in flight, and that is measurable without
writing any code. The fold's launch is `blocks_num(nrows, 1, 1)`, so nrows is
the block count; `-b 512 -ub 1` runs the identical graph at one row per eval.
Same binary, the runner's exact gate command, fold forced on:

    -ub 512 (nrows 512)   3.9364 3.9318 3.9274 3.9418 3.9318 3.9347
                          five distinct values, spread 0.366%
    -ub 1   (nrows 1)     3.9382 x6, spread 0.000%
    -ub 1   fold OFF      3.9382 x3            <- the SAME value
    -ub 512 fold OFF      3.9318 x3

Two things follow. The hazard is between blocks: at one block it is gone. And
at one block the fold is not merely deterministic, it is bit-identical to the
add chain it replaces, which is what it was written to be - so every reading
that has ever been attributed to "the fold reassociates" was the race, not
arithmetic.

The kernel makes the cross-block reading structural. `rms_norm_pre_add_f32`
writes the residual sum to `add_dst` in its first loop and, after only the
block-scope `__syncthreads()` inside `block_reduce`, writes `dst` in its
second. Both buffers legitimately alias operands of the add chain (in-place
residual adds; the host guard only covers the `dst`/`add_dst` pair itself), so
one block's second-loop store is unordered against another block's first-loop
load. Nothing in the kernel orders those two loops across blocks. That also
explains why round 44's register fix did not help: it removed the read-back,
not the write.

This patch takes the half of the fold that is sound. `ggml_cuda_rms_norm_pre_add_detect`
declines above one row, so:

  - decode is always one row - the fold fires and the launch saving is kept;
  - every prefill shape is more than one row, including the gate's 512-row
    ubatch and every 512-token chunk of the long-context windows, so the
    perplexity path runs the parked graph and cannot reach the fold at all.

Measured on the R9700 box, one binary, `GGML_CUDA_DISABLE_PRE_ADD_NORM=1` for
the control, 10 arms interleaved cand/ctl/ctl/cand/cand/ctl/ctl/cand/cand/ctl,
5 llama-bench repetitions each:

    cand tg128  163.78 163.46 164.42 164.74 164.52   mean 164.18
    ctl  tg128  158.58 157.85 159.97 160.13 159.84   mean 159.27   -> +3.08%

5/5 separated (candidate minimum 163.46 above control maximum 160.13).

    cand pp512  4872.6 4791.9 4790.9 4790.2 4809.4   mean 4811.0
    ctl  pp512  4818.1 4824.2 4813.9 4807.2 4803.6   mean 4813.4   -> -0.05%

Prefill is flat by construction: the fold declines at every prefill shape, so
the prefill graph is the parent's.

Correctness, runner's exact gate command (`-c 512 --chunks 8`), candidate at
its shipped default, 4 loads: 3.9318 / 3.9318 / 3.9318 / 3.9318 - the parked
frontier's own value to four decimals with zero spread, against stock 3.9314
(+0.010%). At the decode shape (`-b 512 -ub 1`) candidate reads 3.9382 x3 and
the control 3.9382 x2, i.e. the fold changes nothing it computes.

`GGML_CUDA_DISABLE_PRE_ADD_NORM=1` restores the parked build.
`GGML_CUDA_PRE_ADD_NORM_ALL_ROWS=1` restores the multi-row fold for anyone
continuing the hunt for the residual defect - which is now localised to a
cross-block interaction in a kernel whose only synchronisation is
block-scoped.
---
--- a/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/ggml-cuda.cu
+++ b/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/ggml-cuda.cu
@@ -5247,6 +5247,22 @@
     ggml_tensor * norm     = cgraph->nodes[inorm];
     ggml_tensor * mul      = cgraph->nodes[inorm + 1];
 
+    // One row only.  rms_norm_pre_add_launch issues blocks_num(nrows, 1, 1) and
+    // the kernel writes the residual sum to add_dst in its first loop, then -
+    // after only a block-scope __syncthreads() inside block_reduce - writes dst
+    // in its second.  add_dst and dst legitimately alias operands of the add
+    // chain (in-place residual adds), so with more than one block in flight one
+    // block's second-loop store is unordered against another block's first-loop
+    // load and the result depends on block scheduling.  Nothing in the kernel
+    // orders those two loops across blocks.  At nrows == 1 there is exactly one
+    // block and the hazard cannot exist; see the measurement above the
+    // disable_pre_add_norm gate.
+    static const bool pre_add_all_rows = getenv("GGML_CUDA_PRE_ADD_NORM_ALL_ROWS") != nullptr &&
+                                         std::atoi(getenv("GGML_CUDA_PRE_ADD_NORM_ALL_ROWS"));
+    if (!pre_add_all_rows && ggml_nrows(add_last) != 1) {
+        return 0;
+    }
+
     if (norm->op != GGML_OP_RMS_NORM || norm->src[0] != add_last) {
         return 0;
     }
@@ -6769,17 +6785,25 @@
